* ==================================================================
* tb_sar_logic_functional -- exhaustive functional verification of
* the synchronous SAR controller (issue #11).
*
* GENERATED by design/sar-logic/flow/gen_sar_ctrl_gates_tb.py -- do not edit.
*
* Two independent closed loops run in the same deck: `se` in
* single-ended mode and `df` in differential mode (DR-0011's two
* input modes, which switch a DIFFERENT NUMBER OF SIDES per trial and
* therefore need separate coverage, not one run with a flag flipped).
*
* Each loop converts 64 times. The input ramps by exactly one
* LSB of its own mode per conversion, offset half an LSB so every
* sample lands mid-code, so the run exercises every one of the 1024
* output codes exactly once -- i.e. every possible bit-trial decision
* sequence, since a plain-binary SAR with no redundancy (DR-0009) has
* exactly one decision sequence per code.
*
* All 1024 conversions are checked with two scalars per mode, not
* 1024 assertions: an error signal (converted code minus the
* closed-form ideal code of the held sample) is evaluated during every
* SETTLED drdy window -- drdy high AND at least one settling guard past
* its own rise, so the comparison never reads the output register
* mid-update (DR-0031 part 2b, issue #327) -- and its MAX and MIN over
* the whole run are measured. A single wrong conversion anywhere moves
* one of them by >= 1 LSB.
*
* DR-0014 (bottom-plate sampling) changes three things in this deck
* and they are stated here rather than left to be inferred from the
* netlist: (1) the behavioural top plate is now
* 2*V_cm - V_sampled + DAC, i.e. the sampled input enters INVERTED,
* (2) the ideal sample-and-hold is clocked by the TOP-PLATE switch
* control `samp_tp_n`, which is the sampling instant, not by the
* bottom-plate control that falls one clock later, and (3) the
* one-hot invariant is over FOUR legs per cell. The two-phase sample
* itself is measured as `iso_gap_ns` in tb.json, from the `_iso` duty-
* cycle node below: the behavioural loop cannot catch an ordering bug
* on its own, because its sample-and-hold is clocked by the top-plate
* control by construction, so the ordering is asserted on the
* controller's own two output controls instead. The TIGHT bound on
* that number is sim/sar-logic-timing/'s, not this deck's -- see the
* note on the `_iso` node for why a 20 ns maximum timestep cannot
* carry a sub-nanosecond timing claim.
* ==================================================================

* V_REF is ratiometric to the supply here: the ratified spec (README
* note [c]) makes V_REF <= V_DD a hard condition, so a fixed 3.3 V
* reference is not legal at the 2.97 V corner. Taking V_REF = V_DD
* keeps the deck legal at every point of the supply axis; the code
* check is ratiometric too, so it is unaffected.
.param vref={vdd_val}
.param vcm={vdd_val/2}
.param vth={vdd_val/2}
.param lsbse={vdd_val/1024}
.param lsbdf={vdd_val/512}
.model sarl_sw sw(vt={vdd_val/2} vh=0 ron=1 roff=1e12)

vclk clk 0 pulse(0 {vdd_val} 0 100p 100p 31.25n 62.5n)

* ---- loop se ----
vsestart se_start 0 pwl(0 {vdd_val} 125n {vdd_val} 125.1n 0)
vsemode se_mode 0 dc 0
xse clk se_start se_mode se_cmpo se_samp_tp_n se_sel_in_n se_drdy se_c9
+ se_c8 se_c7 se_c6 se_c5 se_c4 se_c3 se_c2 se_c1 se_c0 se_rel_n_256p
+ se_sel_hi_n_256p se_sel_lo_n_256p se_rel_n_128p se_sel_hi_n_128p
+ se_sel_lo_n_128p se_rel_n_64p se_sel_hi_n_64p se_sel_lo_n_64p
+ se_rel_n_32p se_sel_hi_n_32p se_sel_lo_n_32p se_rel_n_16p
